    `Abraham waited twenty-five years between the promise and the child. Joseph spent thirteen years between the dream and the throne — two of them in a prison for a crime he did not commit. The disciples spent three days between the crucifixion and the resurrection, not knowing that Sunday was coming. Every one of these people lived in the gap — the space between what God had said and what had not yet arrived — and every one of them was required to make a decision about what to do with that gap. The decision was always the same: would they anchor their identity to the promise or to the circumstances? Hebrews 11:1 gives us the biblical definition of faith, and it is almost entirely about this gap. Faith is confidence in what we hope for and assurance about what we do not see. It is not confidence in what has already arrived — that is not faith, that is sight. Faith is the posture you hold in the gap — the settled conviction that what God has spoken is more real than what your circumstances are currently saying, and that the silence of the not-yet does not mean the absence of the God who promised. This is the hardest thing. Not the dramatic crisis of faith where everything is on the line and the stakes are obvious. The slow, grinding, ordinary, undramatic challenge of continuing to trust a God you cannot currently see or feel through the long weeks and months where nothing appears to be happening. Romans 5:3–5 gives us the most important insight into what the gap is actually doing. Suffering — the experience of the not-yet — produces perseverance. Perseverance produces character. Character produces hope. This is not a platitude. It is a description of a process. The hope that does not put us to shame — the unshakeable, proven, tested hope — is not available at the beginning of the wait. It is produced by the wait. The hope that Abraham had at the end of twenty-five years was qualitatively different from the hope he had at the beginning. It had been tested. It had survived years of silence and apparent contradiction. It had become something the circumstances could no longer touch. That is the hope God is building in you through the very gap you are currently impatient to escape.`
