    `Paul asked three times for the thorn to be removed. Three times — which means he brought it to God with earnestness, with persistence, with the kind of repeated petition that Jesus commended in the parable of the persistent widow. He was not asking lazily or halfheartedly. And the answer was no. Not no forever — no, because my grace is sufficient for you, because my power is made perfect in weakness, because the very thing you are asking Me to remove is the condition under which My strength is most completely demonstrated in your life. Paul's thorn was not removed. It was redeemed. That is a different and more remarkable thing. Moses was not allowed to enter the Promised Land. Forty years of wilderness, forty years of leading a people who complained about him, forty years of faithful obedience — and then the Jordan, visible from Nebo, and the word: you will not cross. David was not permitted to build the temple. The man after God's own heart, the one who longed more than anyone to build a house for the name of the Lord — told no. Your son will build it. Not you. These are not small refusals. They are some of the most significant nos in Scripture, delivered to some of the most faithful people in Scripture. And in every case, what looks like deprivation from the outside was actually a specific, sovereign, purposeful act of a God who was building something better than what was being requested. Romans 8:28 is the theological anchor for every divine refusal: God works all things together for good for those who love Him. Not some things. Not the comfortable things, not the things that fit our categories of what good should look like. All things — including the no, including the not yet, including the cup that was not removed and the thorn that was not taken away. This is not a denial that the refusal is painful. It is a declaration that the pain is not purposeless. The God who said no to Paul's request said yes to something infinitely better — the tangible, undeniable, visible manifestation of divine power in human weakness. Paul could not have had both the thorn removed and the power resting on him. God chose the greater gift.`
