    `Elijah stood on Mount Carmel before 450 prophets of Baal, before the entire nation of Israel, and prayed a prayer of forty-three words. Not forty-three paragraphs. Forty-three words. He did not pray for hours. He did not work himself into an emotional state. He simply spoke to God with the clarity of a man who knew exactly who he was talking to and exactly what he was asking — and fire fell from heaven and consumed the sacrifice, the water, the stones, and the soil around the altar. The prophets of Baal had been crying out for hours with increasing desperation. Nothing happened. Elijah prayed forty-three words. Heaven moved. James 5:17 makes a point that is easy to miss: Elijah was a human being, even as we are. The tendency is to read the great prayers of Scripture — Elijah on Carmel, Hannah in the temple, Hezekiah spreading the letter, Daniel in the lion's den — and to quietly categorise them as the prayers of extraordinary people to whom extraordinary things happened. James refuses this categorisation. Elijah was a human being. The same kind of human being you are. What made his prayers effective was not a spiritual superpower unavailable to ordinary believers. It was righteousness, earnestness, and a God who responds to the genuine prayers of genuine people. 2 Chronicles 7:14 gives us the conditions under which God responds: humble yourselves, pray, seek My face, turn from your wicked ways. Four things — and notice their order. Humility comes first. Prayer — the genuine seeking of God's presence and will — comes second. Seeking His face — not His hand, not His gifts, but His face — comes third. And the turning, the repentance, the reorientation of life — that confirms and completes the posture. When these four things are present, God says: then I will hear from heaven, I will forgive, I will heal. The prayer that moves heaven is not the prayer of impressive words or sufficient volume. It is the prayer of a humble, repentant, genuinely seeking heart.`
